.TH CTPOOL_SUBMIT 3 "2026-09-21" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Functions"
.SH NAME
ctpool_submit \- submit a task (blocking when bounded queue is full)
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/cthreadpool.h>

ccol_retval_t ctpool_submit(ctpool pool,
                            void (*fn)(void *),
                            void *arg,
                            void (*on_complete)(void *arg, bool ran));
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Allocates a task struct and adds it to the task queue of the pool. For
an unbounded queue the call returns as soon as the task is in the
queue, and it never blocks on capacity. For a bounded queue the call
blocks until the queue has a free slot.

The worker thread that picks the task up calls fn(arg). The worker
calls on_complete(arg, true) immediately after fn returns, when
on_complete is not NULL. The pool never frees arg. The caller keeps
that ownership.

on_complete runs exactly once for every task that a submit accepts,
and ran says whether fn ran. A task that the pool discards before a
worker takes it gets on_complete(arg, false), and fn never runs.
ctpool_shutdown_immediate(3) discards every task that is still queued,
also when it escalates a ctpool_shutdown_drain(3) that is under way,
and so does a ctpool_destroy(3) of a pool that this process inherited
across fork(2). Those on_complete(arg, false) calls run on the thread
that makes that call, before the call returns, with no lock of the pool
held. on_complete can therefore release arg in both cases. A submit
that fails accepts nothing and never calls on_complete.

An on_complete(arg, false) call counts as a call from the on_complete
callback of a task of the pool, as a call on a worker does. From there,
ctpool_wait(3), ctpool_shutdown_drain(3) and
ctpool_shutdown_immediate(3) on the same pool are no-ops that return at
once, ctpool_destroy(3) on the same pool is a fatal error, and a submit
to the same pool returns ccol_not_permitted, because the pool is in
shutdown (from the ctpool_destroy(3) of a pool inherited across
fork(2) the handle is already retired, so a submit returns
ccol_invalid_args there). This holds for every pool whose discarded callbacks the
thread is running, also when such a callback shuts down a second pool
and the callbacks of that second pool run nested inside it.

The call returns ccol_container_full, and does not wait, in one case.
That case is a call from a task that runs on this same pool, while a
bounded queue is already at capacity. A call from the on_complete
callback of that task is the same case. Only the workers of this pool
can free a slot, and the caller is one of them. Nothing could therefore
ever satisfy the wait.

Use ctpool_try_submit(3) or ctpool_timed_submit(3) when the caller
wants to handle a full queue itself. Use an unbounded queue when the
caller must never get a refusal.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
pool         Thread pool handle
fn           Task function (must not be NULL)
arg          The library gives it to fn and on_complete unchanged
             (may be NULL)
on_complete  Called once for the task: on_complete(arg, true) after
             fn returns, or on_complete(arg, false) when the pool
             discards the task unrun (may be NULL)
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
ccol_success           - task enqueued successfully.
ccol_invalid_args      - pool is CTPOOL_INVALID or a stale/already-destroyed handle, or fn is NULL.
ccol_not_enough_memory - task struct allocation failed.
ccol_not_permitted     - pool is shutting down.
ccol_container_full    - a bounded queue is at capacity and the caller is a worker of this same pool.
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR ctpool_try_submit (3),
.BR ctpool_timed_submit (3),
.BR ctpool_submit_future (3),
.BR ctpool_wait (3)
